{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>May Elohim enlarge Japheth.</b> He blessed Japheth with the widening of boundaries, and the Targum renders (Deuteronomy 12:20) כי ירחיב ארי יפתי — 'when He enlarges your territory'; for even today the descendants of Japheth are countless nations, and their lands are many and broad. And he blessed Shem with the indwelling of the Shekhinah in his tents — this is what it says, וישכן באהלי שם, 'may He dwell in the tents of Shem' — the language of Shekhinah, for there is no nation in the world upon which the Shekhinah rested except Israel, who are of the children of Shem. And in saying באהלי שם he alluded to the Torah, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 25:27) יושב אהלים, 'a dweller in tents'; and this is what it says — וישכן באהלי שם alludes to you that the Shekhinah is drawn after the Torah. And so Shem was blessed with these two blessings: the blessing of the indwelling of the Shekhinah among them, and the blessing of the Torah that would be given to them — what no other nation merited, not even one of the two." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> יפת אלהים ליפת — 'May Elohim enlarge Japheth,' וישכן באהלי שם — 'may He dwell in the tents of Shem.' Even though יפת אלהים ליפת — Japheth was enlarged, that is, Cyrus, who was of the descendants of Japheth, built the Second Temple — וישכן באהלי שם — yet the Shekhinah did not dwell in the Second Temple but only in the First Temple, which Solomon, who was of the children of Shem, built. And so they said in tractate Yoma, chapter one: even though all of them went up in the days of Ezra, the Shekhinah did not rest in the Temple, as it says, יפת אלהים ליפת — even though יפת אלהים ליפת, that is, the Persians who came from Japheth, the Shekhinah dwells only באהלי שם, in the tents of Shem. And from where do we know that the Persians came from Japheth? For it is written (Genesis 10:2) בני יפת — 'the sons of Japheth': Gomer and Magog and Madai and Javan and Tubal and Meshech and Tiras; and Rav Yosef taught: Tiras — this is Persia." "<b>And Canaan shall be a slave to them.</b> The word למו reverts to the brothers he mentioned, that is, to the children of Shem and to the children of Japheth. And the reason he repeated this twice is to strengthen the matter and to establish the slavery of Canaan, and to allude that Shem would inherit his land and all that is his, for what a slave acquires, his master acquires."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:36.497367684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}